IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cpfta-types`

-- Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, Part 3B (ss 27 to 34): offences.
-- Source: source/CPFTA2003.txt lines 1845 to 2194.
--
-- Every offence in this Act is an offence against the Commission's investigation
-- (ss 27 to 30); an unfair practice is not itself an offence.  The statutory maximum
-- for each is a fine of $10,000, or imprisonment of 12 months, or both.

§ `Part 3B -- Offences`

§§ `27 to 30. The four penalties`

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, ss 27(1), 28, 29(3) and 30"
GIVEN k IS A `Kind of Offence`
GIVETH A `Penalty`
`the maximum penalty for the offence` MEANS
    CONSIDER k
    WHEN `an offence of failing to comply with a requirement (section 27)` THEN Penalty OF 10_000, 12
    WHEN `an offence of destroying or falsifying documents (section 28)` THEN Penalty OF 10_000, 12
    WHEN `an offence of giving false or misleading information (section 29)` THEN Penalty OF 10_000, 12
    WHEN `an offence of obstructing an officer of the Commission (section 30)` THEN Penalty OF 10_000, 12

§§ `27. Refusal to provide information, etc.`

-- 27(2): where the requirement is to produce a document, it is a defence to prove that
-- the document was not in the person's possession or under the person's control and
-- that it was not reasonably practicable to comply.

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 27(2)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN r IS A `Refusal Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the defence in section 27(2) is made out` IF
        r's `the requirement is to produce a document`
    AND NOT r's `the document was in the person's possession or under the person's control`
    AND NOT r's `it was reasonably practicable for the person to comply`

-- 27(3): where the requirement is to provide information, an explanation of a
-- document, or a statement of where a document is, it is a defence to prove a
-- reasonable excuse.

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 27(3)(a), (b) and (c)"
GIVEN r IS A `Refusal Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the defence in section 27(3) is made out` IF
        r's `the requirement is to provide information, to provide an explanation of a document, or to state where a document is to be found`
    AND r's `the person had a reasonable excuse for failing to comply`

-- 27(4): failure is not an offence if the person imposing the requirement failed to
-- act in accordance with the section under which it was imposed.

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 27(1) to (4)"
GIVEN r IS A `Refusal Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is guilty of an offence under section 27(1)` IF
        r's `the person fails to comply with a requirement imposed under section 20, 21, 22, 24 or 25`
    AND NOT r's `the person imposing the requirement failed to act in accordance with the section under which it was imposed`
    AND NOT `the defence in section 27(2) is made out` r
    AND NOT `the defence in section 27(3) is made out` r

§§ `28. Destroying or falsifying documents`

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 28(a) and (b)"
GIVEN d IS A `Destruction Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is guilty of an offence under section 28` IF
        d's `the person has been required to produce a document under section 20, 21, 22 or 24`
    AND (   d's `the person intentionally or recklessly destroys, disposes of, falsifies or conceals the document`
         OR d's `the person causes or permits the destruction, disposal, falsification or concealment of the document`)

§§ `29. False or misleading information`

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 29(1), (2)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN f IS A `False Information Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is guilty of an offence under section 29` IF
        (   f's `the person provides information to the Commission, an investigation officer or an authorised assistant in connection with an investigation under section 19(1) or an offence in Part 3B`
         OR f's `the person provides information to another person, knowing it is to be used to provide information to the Commission, an investigation officer or an authorised assistant in connection with a function or duty under the Act`)
    AND f's `the information is false or misleading in a material particular`
    AND (   f's `the person knows the information to be false or misleading in a material particular`
         OR f's `the person is reckless as to whether the information is false or misleading in a material particular`)

§§ `30. Obstructing officer of Commission, etc.`

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 30(a) and (b)"
GIVEN o IS AN `Obstruction Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is guilty of an offence under section 30` IF
        o's `the person obstructs, hinders or impedes a member, officer or employee of the Commission, or an investigation officer or authorised assistant`
    AND o's `the person does so in the discharge of duties or the exercise of powers under the Act or its regulations`
    AND NOT o's `the person has a reasonable excuse`

§§ `31. No costs or damages or other relief arising from seizure recoverable unless seizure without reasonable or probable cause`

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 31"
GIVEN p IS A `Seizure Proceedings Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `costs, damages or other relief is barred` IF
        p's `the proceedings are before a court in respect of equipment, goods, an article or a document seized in the exercise or purported exercise of a power under the Act`
    AND NOT p's `the relief sought is an order for the return of the item or the payment of its value`
    AND NOT p's `the seizure was made without reasonable or probable cause`

§§ `32 and 33. Offences by corporations, unincorporated associations and partnerships`

-- 32(1), 33(1): where an offence needs proof of the body's state of mind, evidence
-- that a person who engaged in the conduct within the scope of actual or apparent
-- authority had that state of mind is evidence that the body had it.  Section 32(1)
-- names an officer, employee or agent of a corporation; s 33(1) names only an
-- employee or agent of an unincorporated association or partnership, and no officer
-- (NOTES.md F6).

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 32(1) and s 33(1)"
GIVEN k IS A `Kind of Body`
      m IS A `State of Mind Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the state of mind of the body is evidenced` IF
        m's `it is necessary in a proceeding for an offence under the Act to prove the state of mind of the body in relation to particular conduct`
    AND m's `that person had that state of mind`
    AND (CONSIDER k
         WHEN `a corporation, including a limited liability partnership` THEN
                m's `an officer of the body engaged in that conduct within the scope of actual or apparent authority`
             OR m's `an employee of the body engaged in that conduct within the scope of actual or apparent authority`
             OR m's `an agent of the body engaged in that conduct within the scope of actual or apparent authority`
         OTHERWISE
                m's `an employee of the body engaged in that conduct within the scope of actual or apparent authority`
             OR m's `an agent of the body engaged in that conduct within the scope of actual or apparent authority`)

-- 32(2), 33(2): the person in a listed role who did one of the listed things is
-- guilty of the same offence as the body, whether or not the body is convicted (32(5),
-- 33(5)).  The listed roles differ by kind of body: for a corporation, an officer
-- (which includes a partner of a limited liability partnership, s 32(6)) or an
-- individual involved in management; for an unincorporated association, an officer or
-- governing-body member or an individual involved in management; for a partnership,
-- a partner or an individual involved in management.

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 32(2)(a) and s 33(2)(a)"
GIVEN b IS A `Body Offence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is in a role that section 32(2) or 33(2) reaches` IF
    CONSIDER b's `kind of body`
    WHEN `a corporation, including a limited liability partnership` THEN
        CONSIDER b's `the person's role`
        WHEN `none of those` THEN FALSE
        OTHERWISE TRUE
    WHEN `an unincorporated association` THEN
        CONSIDER b's `the person's role`
        WHEN `an officer of the corporation, unincorporated association or a member of its governing body` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an individual involved in the management of the body who is in a position to influence its conduct in relation to the commission of the offence` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE
    WHEN `a partnership` THEN
        CONSIDER b's `the person's role`
        WHEN `a partner, or a person purporting to act as a partner` THEN TRUE
        WHEN `an individual involved in the management of the body who is in a position to influence its conduct in relation to the commission of the offence` THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE FALSE

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 32(2)(b)(i), (ii) and (iii) and s 33(2)(b)(i), (ii) and (iii)"
GIVEN b IS A `Body Offence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person did one of the things that section 32(2) or 33(2) reaches` IF
        b's `the person consented or connived, or conspired with others, to effect the commission of the offence`
     OR b's `the person is in any other way, by act or omission, knowingly concerned in or party to the commission of the offence`
     OR (    b's `the person knew or ought reasonably to have known that the offence, or an offence of the same type, would be or is being committed`
         AND b's `the person failed to take all reasonable steps to prevent or stop the commission of the offence`)

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 32(2), (5) and s 33(2), (5)"
GIVEN b IS A `Body Offence Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the person is guilty of the same offence as the body` IF
        b's `the body commits an offence under the Act`
    AND `the person is in a role that section 32(2) or 33(2) reaches` b
    AND `the person did one of the things that section 32(2) or 33(2) reaches` b

-- 32(3), 33(3): the person may rely on a defence available to the body, bearing the
-- same burden of proof.  32(4), 33(4): the Penal Code Chapters 5 and 5A and the
-- Evidence Act are not affected.  32(6), 33(6): definitions of "corporation",
-- "officer", "partner" and "state of mind", taken as facts in the records above.
-- All inert.

§§ `34. Composition of offences`

-- 34(1): the maximum sum is the lower of one half of the maximum fine prescribed for
-- the offence and $5,000.

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 34(1)(a) and (b)"
GIVEN c IS A `Composition Facts`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the most that may be collected in composition` MEANS
    IF c's `the maximum fine prescribed for the offence` DIVIDED BY 2 LESS THAN 5_000
    THEN c's `the maximum fine prescribed for the offence` DIVIDED BY 2
    ELSE 5_000

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 34(1)"
GIVEN c IS A `Composition Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the offence may be compounded for the sum proposed` IF
        c's `the offence is prescribed as a compoundable offence`
    AND c's `the person compounding is the chief executive or an officer of the Commission authorised by the chief executive`
    AND c's `the sum proposed to be collected` AT MOST `the most that may be collected in composition` c

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 34(2)"
GIVEN c IS A `Composition Facts`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `no further proceedings may be taken against the person in respect of the offence` IF
    c's `the sum has been paid`

-- Which offences are compoundable is for regulations under s 43(2)(n).

@ref "Consumer Protection (Fair Trading) Act 2003, s 34(1) and s 43(2)(n)"
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`no regulations under section 43(2)(n) prescribing compoundable offences are encoded in this model` MEANS
    REFUSE "no regulations under section 43(2)(n) prescribing compoundable offences are encoded in this model"

-- 34(3), that sums collected are paid into the Consolidated Fund, and 34(4), that the
-- Commission's members, officers and employees are deemed public officers for the
-- Financial Procedure Act 1966 in administering composition: administrative, inert.
